			<div class="content" id="post-content-3894105">
									

<p>Thank you so much, that's very kind of you to say! I'm delighted you enjoyed it.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;Block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>1) Re: The title. Yeah, that's totally understandable. 'It Won't Stop Screaming' was the initial name I began with when I first came up with the idea. At that point my intention was to focus much more heavily on SCP-4000 and its psychological damage, with more in-depth physical and psychological tests and with the future mystery parts as the flavour surrounding it. In the end, that didn't quite end up working and the mystery became the main focal point of the text with only two references to SCP-4000 constant screaming remaining. However it was pretty close to the deadline by the time I finished and I couldn't think of anything else to call it, so I left the name.</p>
<p>I'm happy to edit it into something a little less edgy if anyone has any good suggestions.</p>
<p>2) Re: Redaction and Expunging. Thank you for your feedback, that's most helpful.<br />
I do indeed know every piece of information I've concealed in this document (with the exception of dates) including what every part of SCP-4000-B originally said so that all the odd letters would be in the right places. But a problem with that is that since I do know everything I don't get to experience what it's like coming to the document without that knowledge.</p>
<p>Part of the frustration at the end is intentional, although my aim was to make it interesting rather than off putting. One of the major focuses of the document is the uncomfortable feeling of knowing that something is coming, but not quite being able to use that knowledge to do anything. The Foundation is teased with knowledge they can't get access to and which might end up being part of a stable time loop, and I wanted to give the reader a little bit of that feeling too, to give them a kind of cliffhanger.</p>
<p>While I was writing this entry my mother got some not very good medical news, so the frustration of 'an unpleasant future you can't do anything to affect and just have to wait for' was very much on my mind.</p>
<p>I should also probably mention that, while it might be a little self-indulgent for my first SCP, part of SCP-4000-B is also foreshadowing. I have a pretty shrewd idea of how Dr. Lecroy ends up where he does and what happens to him on the way, and I'm intending to reveal that story through a number of other SCPs and quite possibly a Tale or two. If I keep to plan - and of course if my work goes down well enough for people to want to see more - every part of B should be explained at some point.</p>
<p>However, that's definitely not an excuse for anything being unclear or off-putting. Any SCP should be able to stand by itself and making it interesting an appealing by itself is more important than lore building.</p>
<p>I can't really change the [DATA EXPUNGED] in Carter's second report because it would end up referencing something that wasn't very clear to the reader anyway, and if I elaborated further it would probably end up feeling like I'd gone off on a complete tangent since it doesn't have a clear link to SCP-4000's condition.</p>
<p>However, given that two people have found the redaction at the very end confusing I'm considering removing it, or at least part of it.</p>
<p>The reason I've picked on Site-41 is that I found it to be the most enjoyably inconsistent, canon-conflicting Foundation facility of all the sites I looked at. I was searching out if any Sites had a particular theme to them (like 17's connection to psychology, which is why I put SCP-4000 there) when I came upon some references to Site-41.</p>
<p>So far, I have discovered that it is in a frigid arctic landscape and has an SCP containment greenhouse. It's home to a major operations team within the Foundation and is also virtually devoid of people and partly abandoned. It been abandoned and replaced with Site-41-B, and also destroyed in a completely different way. It was constructed SCP-567 and also <em>is</em> SCP-1312. It is located in Japan, and also located in Leningrad during WWII.</p>
<p>I know there isn't just one Foundation canon, and that differences can also be put down to 'misinformation' but Site-41 attracted me as being almost intentionally chaotic. I thought it merited a reason for its many, many contradictions. And that is, currently, what I believe the final redaction covers. If I were going to remove it, I'd either take it out all together or change it to a comment about how data on Site-41 is confusing to ascertain but that official Foundation records state that the current Site-41 is indeed the only one there has ever been.</p>
<p><strong>I'll leave it for the moment, but if anyone else has any feedback on the ending and thinks changing it would be a good idea, please let me know.</strong></p>
<p>Thank you.</p>
