			<div class="content" id="post-content-3905206">
									

<p>Thank you very much for the feedback, I'm glad I made the change.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Ending&nbsp;Thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Ending&nbsp;Thinkings</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I can definitely see where you're coming from about the new ending point. It was actually originally ordered slightly differently, though I believe the proposals were always at the bottom of that section. It's definitely not as punch as &quot;The importance of restoring SCP-4000’s mind has been upgraded to Priority-1&quot; would be, but I don't think it makes a ton of sense any earlier in that second, and that information is more or less is in the SCProcedures already.</p>
<p>The reason I included it at the end was an attempt to a) clarify the earlier references a bit and b) to make the article cyclical, which I thought was appropriate for a semi-stable time loop SCP. It ends with the information that it effectively starts with.</p>
<p>If I was going to alter the final part to finish on Priority-1, I think I'd probably just have to scrap the final explanatory lines. I'm a bit loath to do so though because I feel they do an OK job emphasising that, hey, this new mysterious information is important. Priority-1 does that a bit, but since I just made up the priority system it doesn't necessarily tell the reader exactly how seriously this is being taken.</p>
<p>At least, I've made up this priority system. I don't believe the Foundation already has one, but I may well have missed it.</p>
<p>My system is basically a concept for how resource allocation works based on how important research is. Priority-0 is &quot;If this isn't done in two hours the universe may implode&quot;, Priority-1 is &quot;Get this done as soon as possible, throw all the resources at&quot;, Priority-2 is &quot;This is very important but doesn't have a real timescale or a very long one, so have lots of resources and do what you can but no need to rush&quot;, Priority-3 is &quot;This is highly important, it would be wonderful to get done, but we're not going to collapse if it doesn't succeed&quot;, Priority-4 is &quot;This is something we should be working on, but its unlikely to succeed or the benefits aren't worth excessive cost&quot;, Priority-5 is &quot;This is a good thing to work on, but not that important&quot;, and Priority-6 is &quot;Meh. Do it. Don't do it. No one's going to be riding you about this one. Just put in some research notes every few days.&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<strong>Anyway, I think I'll leave the ending the same for the moment but I would really appreciate thoughts on the matter</strong>
							</div>

								
					
